# CSS Modules and styling

Use CSS Modules for component styles. Do not add Tailwind, Sass, or CSS-in-JS. Prefer the bundler's built-in `*.module.css` support.

## Dependency direction

Styles flow one way. Never reverse:

```
tokens → reset/base → shared primitives → colocated component modules
```

Map SOLID onto that pipeline:

- **SRP** — one `.module.css` per component; one class, one job (`root`, `title`, `actions`). Tokens, reset, and components live in separate files.
- **OCP** — extend with variants, `composes`, or new token values. Do not edit a shared primitive to satisfy one screen.
- **LSP** — composed classes must be safe substitutes. Do not `composes` two classes from different files that set the same property (cross-file apply order is undefined).
- **ISP** — export a small class API. No kitchen-sink class that every sibling depends on.
- **DIP** — components depend on semantic custom properties (`--color-text`, `--space-md`), never raw palette values or another component's internal classes.

Shared primitives compose **into** components. Components never compose from each other.

## File roles

Describe files by role, not by a fixed app tree:

| Role | Owns | Does not own |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Global entry | `@layer` order and `@import`s only | Component visuals |
| Tokens | Palette vars and semantic aliases | Selectors for UI |
| Reset / base | Box-sizing, `html`/`body`, focus, element defaults, fonts | Component class names |
| Primitives | Reusable layout or type classes, wrapped in `@layer` | Screen-specific look |
| Component module | One component's visuals and internal layout | Global resets or tokens |

Colocate `Component.tsx` with `Component.module.css`. Put tokens, reset, and primitives beside each other under a styles directory (for example `styles/tokens.css`, `styles/reset.css`, `styles/primitives.module.css`).

## Global vs module

**Global:** cascade layer order, CSS variables, document defaults, fonts.

**Module:** visual treatment and internal layout of one component.

`:global` only for third-party hooks or document-level state. Never wrap a whole module in `:global`.

## Cascade layers

Declare a single order in the global entry:

```css
@layer reset, tokens, base, primitives;
```

Wrap shared styles in those layers. Leave colocated component modules **unlayered**. Unlayered rules beat layered ones at equal specificity, so a local module can override a primitive without `!important` or extra specificity.

## Tokens

Two-tier values for color, space, and type.

- Palette (never used in components): `--color-green-700`, `--space-8`
- Semantic (what components use): `--color-text`, `--color-surface`, `--color-accent`, `--space-md`

```css
@layer tokens {
  :root {
    --color-green-700: oklch(40% 0.08 150);
    --color-text: var(--color-green-700);
    --space-8: 0.5rem;
    --space-md: var(--space-8);
  }
}
```

No magic numbers for color, spacing, or type in modules. Use `var(--...)`.

## CSS Modules mechanics

- camelCase class names so `styles.root` works with dot access
- `import styles from './Button.module.css'`
- `composes` must be the first declarations in a rule; no circular `composes`
- Compose shared primitives instead of copying declarations
- Nesting at most two levels
- No IDs and no `!important`
- State and exceptions via `data-*` so JS and CSS share one hook: `[data-state='open']`, `[data-variant='primary']`
- Visual variants may also be extra classes combined in TSX

```css
/* primitives.module.css */
@layer primitives {
  .stack {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-md);
  }
}
```

```css
/* Button.module.css */
.root {
  composes: stack from '../styles/primitives.module.css';
  padding: var(--space-md);
  color: var(--color-text);
  background: var(--color-surface);
}

.root[data-variant='primary'] {
  background: var(--color-accent);
  color: var(--color-surface);
}

.root[data-state='disabled'] {
  opacity: 0.5;
}

.label {
  font: inherit;
}
```

## Agent do / don't

**Do**

- Colocate a module with its component
- Create token / reset / primitive files when they are missing, then use them
- Keep selectors shallow
- Compose primitives; override in the unlayered local module
- Verify visual changes in the browser

**Don't**

- Dump global CSS in a `<style>` tag or `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`
- Add Tailwind, Sass, or CSS-in-JS
- Import another component's `.module.css` to style this one
- Use unscoped global class names for components (`.button`, `.wrapper`)
- Reach for palette vars or raw hex/rgb in a component module

## Good / bad

```tsx
// Good
import styles from './Button.module.css';

type ButtonProps = {
  variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary';
  disabled?: boolean;
  children: React.ReactNode;
};

export function Button({
  variant = 'secondary',
  disabled,
  children,
}: ButtonProps) {
  return (
    <button
      type="button"
      className={styles.root}
      data-variant={variant}
      data-state={disabled ? 'disabled' : undefined}
      disabled={disabled}
    >
      <span className={styles.label}>{children}</span>
    </button>
  );
}
```

```tsx
// Bad — unscoped names, magic numbers, sibling-module coupling
import cardStyles from '../Card/Card.module.css';

export function Button({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return (
    <button className={`button ${cardStyles.root}`} style={{ color: '#228B22' }}>
      {children}
    </button>
  );
}
```

```css
/* Bad — composes conflicting properties from two files; kebab-case; :global leak */
.cta-button {
  composes: stack from '../styles/primitives.module.css';
  composes: padded from '../Card/Card.module.css';
}

:global(.button) {
  padding: 12px;
  color: #228b22;
}
```
